Explanation:
1. Guard g("f") is created inside f().
2. throw 42 throws an exception.
3. During stack unwinding, g is destroyed first, printing ~f.
4. The Guard m("main") object is then destroyed, printing ~main.
5. The catch(int x) handler catches the exception and prints caught 42.
6. "not printed" does not appear because the exception transfers control away from that statement.

Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
If resources were released using delete inside every catch block instead of RAII, every error-handling path would need to manually release allocated resources.
Feature	RAII	Manual delete in Catch Blocks
Time complexity	Same	Same
Code size	Less code	More code
Resource release	Automatic	Manual
Leak risk	Low	Higher
New error paths	Safe automatically	Every path must be checked

Conclusion: RAII is better because resources are automatically released during stack unwinding. It reduces code complexity and prevents memory leaks when new error paths are added.
